\id GAL - Restored Context Bible (RCB)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h Galatians
\toc1 The Letter of Paul to the Assemblies in Galatia
\toc2 Galatians
\toc3 Gal
\mt1 Galatians

\c 1
\p
\v 1 Paul, one sent\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Greek \tl apostolos\tl*, from which "apostle" comes: an emissary who carries the authority of the one who sent him.\f* \add [i.e. carrying the full authority of the one who sent me]\add* — not from men \add [i.e. my commission did not originate with any human council]\add*, nor through a man \add [i.e. nor did it reach me through any human go-between]\add*, but through Jesus the Christ and God the Father, who raised him from among the dead \add [— and by raising him made him the Lord whose commission I carry]\add* —
\v 2 and all the brothers with me \add [who stand behind what I write, though the argument here is mine]\add*, to the assemblies of Galatia \add [i.e. to every one of them — this trouble has run through the whole region]\add*:
\p
\v 3 Favor to you and peace\f + \fr 1:3 \ft Greek \tl eirēnē\tl*, standing for Hebrew \tl shalom\tl*: not merely the absence of conflict but wholeness and well-being.\f* from God our Father and the Lord Jesus the Christ,
\v 4 who gave himself \add [i.e. handed himself over as a sacrifice]\add* for our sins so that he might snatch us out of the present evil age\f + \fr 1:4 \ft Greek \tl exaireō\tl*, the verb of the exodus rescue: "I have come down to deliver them out of the hand of the Egyptians" (Exodus 3:8 LXX).\f* \add [i.e. this whole present order, held by powers hostile to God, which the age to come will replace — and the rescue is already accomplished]\add*, according to the will of our God and Father \add [— this was no improvisation; God had determined it beforehand]\add*,
\v 5 to whom be the glory into the ages of the ages \add [i.e. for ever, without end]\add*. Amen.
\p
\v 6 I am astonished \add [— where any letter of mine would begin by thanking God for you, I cannot]\add* that you are so quickly turning away\f + \fr 1:6 \ft Greek \tl metatithesthe\tl*, used of soldiers changing sides and deserting to the enemy. The tense is present: the desertion is under way, not finished.\f* from the one who called you \add [i.e. God himself — it is him you are leaving, not merely my teaching]\add* in the favor of the Christ, to a different good news \add [i.e. one of another kind altogether: that you must be circumcised and take on the Sinai covenant to have standing before God]\add* —
\v 7 which is not another\f + \fr 1:7 \ft Two different Greek words: \tl heteron\tl* in v. 6, another of a different kind; \tl allo\tl* here, another of the same kind.\f* \add [i.e. there is no second good news standing beside the one you received]\add*; only there are some \add [i.e. certain men who came in after I left]\add* who are stirring you up and want to overturn \add [i.e. turn into its opposite]\add* the good news of the Christ.
\v 8 But even if we \add [i.e. I myself, or any of us who first brought it to you]\add*, or a messenger out of heaven \add [i.e. an angel sent from God's own presence]\add*, should announce good news to you other than what we announced to you\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Compare Deuteronomy 13:1-5, where a prophet is judged by his message and not by his credentials — even when his signs come true.\f*, let him be under the ban\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Greek \tl anathema\tl*, from Hebrew \tl cherem\tl*, the irrevocable ban of total destruction (Leviticus 27:28-29).\f*.
\v 9 As we have said before \add [i.e. when I was still with you]\add*, so now I say again: if anyone announces good news to you other than what you received \add [from me]\add*, let him be under the ban\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Verse 8 puts the case as a supposition; here the Greek shifts to a statement of fact. Someone is doing this now.\f*.
\p
\v 10 For am I now persuading \add [i.e. courting the favor of]\add* men, or God? Or am I seeking to please men? If I were still pleasing men \add [as I did in the days when I was making my name in Judaism]\add*, I would not be a slave of the Christ \add [i.e. a man owned outright by his master, with no other loyalty left to give]\add*.
\p
\v 11 For I make known to you, brothers, that the good news announced by me is not according to man \add [i.e. it is no human invention, cut to a human pattern]\add*.
\v 12 For I did not receive it from a man\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Greek \tl paralambanō\tl*, the technical word for taking over a tradition handed down from a teacher. The authority of a teaching rested on that chain of transmission; Paul places himself outside it entirely.\f*, nor was I taught it \add [by any human teacher, and least of all by those in Jerusalem]\add*, but through an uncovering of Jesus the Christ \add [i.e. the risen Christ himself was unveiled to me, and the message with him]\add*.
\p
\v 13 For you have heard how I once walked in Judaism\f + \fr 1:13 \ft Greek \tl Ioudaismos\tl*, the word used in 2 Maccabees for the militant practice and defense of the ancestral Jewish way of life against Hellenizing pressure.\f* \add [i.e. the whole zealous program of ancestral observance — not my ancestry, but my cause]\add* — that beyond all measure I was hunting down the assembly of God and laying it waste \add [— a sustained campaign, the way an army lays waste a city]\add*,
\v 14 and I was pushing ahead in Judaism beyond many of my own age among my people, being far more zealous\f + \fr 1:14 \ft Zeal of this kind followed the pattern of Phinehas (Numbers 25:6-13), invoked again by Mattathias in 1 Maccabees 2:26, 54: zeal for the Torah that takes up violence against those who violate it.\f* for the traditions of my fathers \add [i.e. the whole body of ancestral custom handed down alongside the written Torah — the very traditions these men are now pressing on you]\add*.
\p
\v 15 But when God, who set me apart from my mother's womb\f + \fr 1:15 \ft The language of the prophetic call: Jeremiah 1:5 ("before I formed you in the womb I knew you... I appointed you a prophet to the nations") and Isaiah 49:1, 6.\f* and called me through his favor \add [i.e. not for any qualification of mine — what I had qualified me to persecute]\add*, was pleased
\v 16 to uncover his Son in me \add [i.e. in me and through me]\add*, so that I might announce him as good news among the nations \add [i.e. the Gentile peoples — this was the purpose of my calling from before I was born]\add*, I did not at once lay it before flesh and blood \add [i.e. put it to any human being for approval]\add*,
\v 17 nor did I go up to Jerusalem to those sent before me \add [i.e. the apostles already in place there]\add*, but I went away into Arabia \add [i.e. the Nabatean kingdom east of Damascus]\add* and turned back again to Damascus \add [where the Lord had met me]\add*.
\p
\v 18 Then after three years \add [i.e. three years of announcing this good news with no contact with the leaders in Jerusalem]\add* I went up to Jerusalem to get to know Cephas \add [i.e. Peter]\add*, and I stayed with him fifteen days \add [— time enough to become acquainted, not to be taught a gospel]\add*.
\v 19 But I saw none of the other sent ones \add [i.e. none of the other apostles]\add* except James the brother of the Lord \add [i.e. Jesus' own brother, who had become a leader in Jerusalem]\add*.
\v 20 In what I am writing to you — look, before the face of God — I am not lying \add [— my account of these years is being disputed, and I put it under oath]\add*.
\p
\v 21 Then I went into the regions of Syria and Cilicia \add [i.e. north, away from Judea altogether — Syria, whose chief city is Antioch, and Cilicia, my own home country]\add*.
\v 22 And I was unknown by face to the assemblies of Judea that are in the Christ \add [— the communities nearest the apostles had never so much as seen me]\add*.
\v 23 They were only hearing that "the one who once hunted us down is now announcing as good news the faith \add [i.e. the message and the movement itself]\add* he once laid waste,"
\v 24 and they were glorifying God in me \add [i.e. for what God had done in me — they did not summon me, or question me, or require me to present myself for approval; they praised God]\add*.

\c 2
\p
\v 1 Then after fourteen years \add [i.e. fourteen more years of this work before I set foot there again]\add* I went up again to Jerusalem with Barnabas \add [i.e. my partner in the mission, who was there and saw all of it]\add*, taking Titus along with me as well.
\v 2 I went up according to an uncovering \add [i.e. because God disclosed to me that I should go — I was not summoned]\add*, and I laid before them the good news that I announce among the nations — privately, though, before those reputed to be something\f + \fr 2:2 \ft Greek \tl hoi dokountes\tl*, "those who seem to be." Paul grants their standing without granting that it adds anything to his message; he repeats the phrase in vv. 6 and 9.\f* — so that I might not somehow be running, or have run, into emptiness \add [i.e. so that a rupture with them would not rob my work among the nations of its effect]\add*.
\v 3 But not even Titus, who was with me, was compelled to be circumcised, Greek though he was \add [i.e. an uncircumcised Gentile, and every man there knew it — the disputed question standing in front of them in the flesh, and they did not require it of him]\add*.
\v 4 And that was because of the false brothers smuggled in \add [i.e. men who came in wearing the name of brother and carrying another errand]\add*, who slipped in to spy out\f + \fr 2:4 \ft Greek \tl kataskopeō\tl*, a military word for reconnoitering enemy ground — the word used of the men sent to spy out Jericho (Joshua 2:1 LXX).\f* our freedom that we have in the Christ Jesus \add [i.e. that we need not come into the Sinai covenant by circumcision to stand before God]\add*, so that they might bring us down into slavery —
\v 5 to them we did not yield in submission, not even for an hour, so that the truth of the good news might remain with you \add [i.e. so that what reached you would be the whole of it, with nothing added]\add*.
\p
\v 6 And from those reputed to be something — what sort they once were makes no difference to me; God does not receive the face of a man\f + \fr 2:6 \ft A Semitism: Hebrew \tl nasa panim\tl*, "to lift up the face," meaning to show favor on the strength of a man's standing. God does not (Deuteronomy 10:17; Leviticus 19:15).\f* — those reputed to be something laid nothing further on me \add [i.e. they added not one requirement to what I set before them]\add*.
\v 7 On the contrary, when they saw that I had been entrusted with the good news of the uncircumcision \add [i.e. the one good news, given me to carry to the uncircumcised nations]\add*, just as Peter with that of the circumcision \add [i.e. the same message, given him to carry to the Jews]\add* —
\v 8 for the one who worked in Peter \add [i.e. God himself]\add* for a sending to the circumcision worked also in me for the nations —
\v 9 and when they knew the favor given to me, James and Cephas and John, those reputed to be pillars \add [i.e. the men on whom the assembly there is held up]\add*, gave to me and Barnabas the right hands of partnership\f + \fr 2:9 \ft The formal clasp of the right hand sealed an alliance between equals (1 Maccabees 6:58; 2 Maccabees 12:11-12).\f*, that we should go to the nations and they to the circumcision \add [i.e. a division of ground, not a division of message]\add*.
\v 10 Only \add [— and this was the whole of what they asked]\add*, that we should remember the poor \add [i.e. the destitute among the believers in Jerusalem]\add* — the very thing I was eager to do.
\p
\v 11 But when Cephas came to Antioch\f + \fr 2:11 \ft The chief city of Syria and the base of the mission to the nations, where Jewish and Gentile believers ate at one table.\f*, I stood against him to his face, because he stood condemned \add [i.e. his own conduct had already passed sentence on him]\add*.
\v 12 For before certain men came from James, he was eating with the nations \add [i.e. sharing the table with Gentile believers, which said in plain sight that they belonged]\add*; but when they came, he began to draw back and separate himself, fearing those of the circumcision \add [i.e. the party that held Gentiles must be circumcised to belong]\add*.
\v 13 And the rest of the Jews played the same part with him\f + \fr 2:13 \ft Greek \tl hypokrisis\tl* and its verb belong to the theater: to play a role that is not one's own. They knew better than they acted.\f*, so that even Barnabas \add [i.e. my own partner, who had stood beside me in Jerusalem]\add* was carried away with them in their play-acting.
\v 14 But when I saw that their feet were not walking straight toward the truth of the good news, I said to Cephas in front of them all \add [— the withdrawal was public, so the rebuke was public]\add*: "If you, being a Jew, live in the way of the nations and not in the way of the Jews, how is it that you compel the nations to live in the way of the Jews\f + \fr 2:14 \ft Greek \tl Ioudaizein\tl*, "to Judaize": for a Gentile to take on the Jewish way of life as a proselyte (Esther 8:17 LXX). It means conversion, not the borrowing of a few customs.\f* \add [— for that is what pulling away from their table says to them]\add*?"
\p
\v 15 We are Jews by nature and not sinners out of the nations \add [— to use the word our own people use of those outside the covenant]\add*;
\v 16 but knowing that a man is not set right\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Greek \tl dikaioō\tl*, a courtroom word: to be declared in the right by the judge.\f* out of works of Torah\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Greek \tl erga nomou\tl*: the obligations that mark out membership in the Sinai covenant, circumcision first among them. It is not Torah's moral instruction, which Paul goes on to teach and command (5:14; 6:2).\f* but through the faithfulness of Jesus the Christ\f + \fr 2:16 \ft The Greek \tl pistis Iēsou Christou\tl* can be read as the Christ's own faithfulness or as our trust in him. Both are at work here, and the sentence goes on to say the second outright.\f*, we too \add [i.e. we who were born inside the covenant and had every advantage it gave]\add* put our trust into the Christ Jesus, so that we might be set right out of the faithfulness of the Christ and not out of works of Torah — because out of works of Torah no flesh \add [i.e. no human being at all]\add* will be set right\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Echoing Psalm 143:2 (LXX 142:2), "no one living will be set right before you." Where the psalm said "before you," Paul names what cannot do it: works of Torah.\f*.
\v 17 But if, while seeking to be set right in the Christ, we ourselves were found to be sinners \add [i.e. found standing outside the covenant, in the very category we assign to the nations]\add*, is the Christ then a servant of sin? May it never be!\f + \fr 2:17 \ft Greek \tl mē genoito\tl*, the strongest refusal the language has. Paul uses it where an argument is logically built but monstrous.\f*
\v 18 For if I build again the things I tore down \add [i.e. if I put back the covenant system I gave up as the ground of my standing]\add*, I stand myself up as one who steps over the line \add [— the offense is not in leaving it, but in going back to it]\add*.
\v 19 For through Torah I died to Torah\f + \fr 2:19 \ft How the Torah itself produced this death is stated at 3:13: the Torah pronounced its curse on the crucified one, and when that curse fell on the Christ its claim was spent on all who are joined to him.\f*, so that I might live to God. I have been crucified together with the Christ;
\v 20 and I no longer live, but the Christ lives in me. And the life I now live in flesh \add [i.e. in this mortal body]\add*, I live in the faithfulness of the Son of God, who loved me and handed himself over for me \add [— what I said of us all at the opening of this letter, I say here of myself alone]\add*.
\v 21 I do not set aside the favor of God; for if righteousness is through Torah \add [i.e. if entering that covenant could put a man right with God]\add*, then the Christ died for nothing.

\c 3
\p
\v 1 O foolish Galatians \add [i.e. you are not dull; you have simply stopped thinking]\add*! Who put the evil eye on you\f + \fr 3:1 \ft Greek \tl baskainō\tl*, to bewitch or cast the evil eye — a spell that stops a man from seeing what stands in front of him.\f* — you before whose own eyes Jesus the Christ was posted up in public \add [i.e. placarded as plainly as a public notice nailed up in the square]\add*, crucified?
\v 2 This alone I want to learn from you: did you receive the Spirit \add [i.e. when he came on you, unmistakably, and you all knew it]\add* out of works of Torah \add [i.e. were you circumcised first? were you brought into the covenant first?]\add*, or out of hearing and trusting?
\v 3 Are you so foolish? Having begun with Spirit, are you now being finished off with flesh \add [i.e. brought to completion by the circumcising knife]\add*?
\v 4 Did you go through so much \add [i.e. all you suffered for this, and all you saw God do among you]\add* for nothing — if indeed it really was for nothing?
\v 5 So then, the one who supplies the Spirit to you and works acts of power among you \add [i.e. God, and he is doing it still, among uncircumcised men]\add* — is it out of works of Torah, or out of hearing and trusting?
\p
\v 6 Just as Abraham trusted God, and it was counted to him as righteousness\f + \fr 3:6 \ft Genesis 15:6. The reckoning comes two chapters before circumcision is given (Genesis 17): Abraham was declared righteous while still uncircumcised.\f* —
\v 7 know then that those who are out of faithfulness \add [i.e. those whose standing rests on trust, as his did]\add*, these are sons of Abraham.
\v 8 And the Scripture, seeing beforehand that God sets the nations right out of faithfulness, announced the good news beforehand to Abraham \add [i.e. the very announcement I now carry was spoken to him in advance]\add*: "In you all the nations will be blessed."\f + \fr 3:8 \ft Genesis 12:3; 18:18. The blessing of the nations belongs to the promise made to Abraham, centuries before Sinai.\f*
\v 9 So then, those who are out of faithfulness are blessed together with faithful Abraham.
\p
\v 10 For as many as are out of works of Torah \add [i.e. all whose standing rests on entry into that covenant]\add* are under a curse; for it is written, "Cursed is everyone who does not remain in all the things written in the scroll of the Torah, to do them."\f + \fr 3:10 \ft Deuteronomy 27:26. The curse is the covenant's own machinery, falling on its members for anything short of all — and no one renders all.\f*
\v 11 And that no one is set right before God in Torah is clear, because "The righteous one shall live out of faithfulness."\f + \fr 3:11 \ft Habakkuk 2:4.\f*
\v 12 But the Torah is not out of faithfulness; rather, "The one who does them shall live in them."\f + \fr 3:12 \ft Leviticus 18:5. The two texts name two different principles for how a man comes to stand before God: the one, trust; the other, performance.\f*
\v 13 The Christ bought us \add [i.e. us who were inside that covenant and under its sentence]\add* out from the curse of the Torah, becoming a curse for us — because it is written, "Cursed is everyone who hangs on a tree"\f + \fr 3:13 \ft Deuteronomy 21:23. By being crucified, the Christ entered the exact category the Torah had named accursed, and the curse fell there.\f* —
\v 14 so that the blessing of Abraham might come to the nations in the Christ Jesus, so that we \add [i.e. all of us now, Jew and Gentile together]\add* might receive the promise of the Spirit \add [i.e. the Spirit himself, who is the thing promised]\add* through faithfulness.
\p
\v 15 Brothers, I speak in a human way \add [i.e. I take my illustration from ordinary dealings among men]\add*: even a man's covenant, once it has been ratified, no one sets aside or adds to.
\v 16 Now the promises were spoken to Abraham and to his seed. It does not say, "and to seeds," as of many, but as of one: "and to your seed"\f + \fr 3:16 \ft Genesis 13:15; 17:8; 22:18. The word is singular in the Hebrew and in the Greek alike.\f* — who is the Christ.
\v 17 This I say: a covenant previously ratified by God is not annulled by the Torah, which came four hundred and thirty years later\f + \fr 3:17 \ft The figure comes from Exodus 12:40.\f* \add [i.e. the promise was in force long before Sinai, and what comes later cannot add terms to it]\add*, so as to make the promise idle.
\v 18 For if the inheritance is out of Torah, it is no longer out of a promise; but God has given it to Abraham as a gift \add [i.e. freely, out of his own favor, with nothing owed]\add* through a promise.
\p
\v 19 Why then the Torah? It was added because of transgressions \add [i.e. to make wrongdoing plain as the breaking of named commands]\add*, until the seed should come to whom the promise had been made \add [— it was given with a term set on it from the start]\add* — put in order through messengers, in the hand of a go-between.\f + \fr 3:19 \ft The messengers are angels, by whom the Torah was held to have been delivered at Sinai (Deuteronomy 33:2 LXX; Acts 7:53; Hebrews 2:2); the go-between is Moses, who stood between the LORD and the people (Deuteronomy 5:5).\f*
\v 20 Now a go-between is not of one, but God is one.\f + \fr 3:20 \ft A go-between stands between two parties, and the Sinai covenant had two. The promise had one: God bound himself alone while Abraham slept (Genesis 15). "God is one" is the Shema, Deuteronomy 6:4.\f*
\v 21 Is the Torah then against the promises of God? May it never be! For if a Torah had been given that was able to make alive \add [i.e. able to give life, and not only to govern it]\add*, then righteousness really would have been out of Torah.
\v 22 But the Scripture shut up everything together under sin \add [i.e. left no man any way out but the promise]\add*, so that the promise out of the faithfulness of Jesus the Christ might be given to those who trust.
\p
\v 23 But before the faithfulness came, we \add [i.e. we Jews, who lived inside that covenant]\add* were kept under guard under Torah, shut up until the faithfulness that was about to come \add [i.e. this present time of trust, which arrived with the Christ]\add* should be uncovered.
\v 24 So the Torah became our guardian\f + \fr 3:24 \ft Greek \tl paidagōgos\tl*: not a teacher but a household slave who walked a boy to school, kept him in order in public, and enforced discipline. His authority was real, borrowed from the father, and it ended the day the boy came of age.\f* until the Christ, so that we might be set right out of faithfulness.
\v 25 But now that the faithfulness has come, we are no longer under a guardian \add [i.e. we for whom he was appointed are out from under him — and you were never under him at all]\add*.
\v 26 For you \add [i.e. you Gentiles, who never came under that guardian]\add* are all sons of God through the faithfulness in the Christ Jesus.
\v 27 For as many of you as were immersed into the Christ have clothed yourselves with the Christ \add [i.e. he is what you now wear before the world, in place of every older marking]\add*.
\v 28 There is no Jew nor Greek, there is no slave nor free, there is no male and female\f + \fr 3:28 \ft The wording "male and female" is lifted from Genesis 1:27. It carries weight here because circumcision could mark only one of the two.\f*; for you are all one in the Christ Jesus \add [i.e. not that the differences vanish, but that not one of them counts toward standing before God]\add*.
\v 29 And if you are of the Christ \add [— and you are]\add*, then you are Abraham's seed, heirs according to promise \add [i.e. already, and by promise — there is nothing left for circumcision to add]\add*.

\c 4
\p
\v 1 Now I say, for as long as the heir is a child \add [i.e. under age, not yet come into his estate]\add*, he is no different from a slave, though he is lord of everything,
\v 2 but he is under trustees and stewards until the day set beforehand by the father \add [i.e. the father fixes that day, not the trustees and not the boy]\add*.
\v 3 So also we \add [i.e. we Jews, before the Christ came]\add*, when we were children, were enslaved under the elemental things of the world.\f + \fr 4:3 \ft Greek \tl stoicheia\tl*, the "first things" — the letters of the alphabet, the elements of the world. Paul uses it for the whole order of external regulation under which men lived before the Christ: for Jews the covenant's calendar and ritual, for Gentiles the worship of the powers. The two differ utterly in content and function identically, and that is his point in vv. 8-9.\f*
\v 4 But when the fullness of the time came \add [i.e. when the day the Father had set arrived]\add*, God sent out his Son, born of a woman, born under Torah \add [i.e. inside that same covenant, subject to everything it required]\add*,
\v 5 so that he might buy out those under Torah, so that we \add [i.e. all of us, and not only those he bought out]\add* might receive the adoption as sons.\f + \fr 4:5 \ft Greek \tl huiothesia\tl*, the legal act of placing a person into a family as a son, with the full rights of an heir.\f*
\v 6 And because you are sons \add [i.e. the Spirit came because you were sons already; he did not make you sons]\add*, God sent out the Spirit of his Son into our hearts, crying, "Abba! Father!"\f + \fr 4:6 \ft \tl Abba\tl* is the Aramaic word a child uses of his father at home, kept here with its Greek translation beside it (compare Mark 14:36).\f*
\v 7 So you are no longer a slave but a son; and if a son, then also an heir through God.
\p
\v 8 But then, not knowing God, you were slaves to those which by nature are not gods \add [i.e. the gods of your old worship, which are no gods at all]\add*.
\v 9 But now, having come to know God — or rather, having been known by God \add [i.e. he found you; you did not find him]\add* — how is it that you are turning back again \add [— back, for though the Torah is God's own and your idols were nothing, to make either one the price of access to God is the same bondage]\add* to the weak and poor elemental things \add [i.e. bankrupt: they demand everything and can deliver nothing]\add*, to which you want to be enslaved all over again?
\v 10 You are watching days and months and seasons and years.\f + \fr 4:10 \ft The sabbaths and appointed days, the new moons, the yearly feasts, and the sabbatical years — the covenant calendar. They have taken it up already, before any circumcision.\f*
\v 11 I am afraid for you, that somehow I have labored over you for nothing.
\p
\v 12 Become as I am \add [i.e. free of that covenant's claim as the ground of your standing]\add*, because I also became as you are \add [i.e. a Jew who lived among you as one of you, and asked nothing of you that I would not lay down myself]\add*, brothers, I beg you. You did me no wrong.
\v 13 You know that it was because of a weakness of the flesh \add [i.e. a bodily illness, which is what halted me among you in the first place]\add* that I announced the good news to you the first time,
\v 14 and though my flesh was a trial to you, you did not despise me or spit me out\f + \fr 4:14 \ft Spitting was the common gesture for warding off the contagion, or the ill fortune, that a sick man was thought to carry.\f*, but you received me as a messenger of God \add [i.e. as an angel sent from him]\add*, as the Christ Jesus.
\v 15 Where then is your blessing \add [i.e. the gladness you had in those days]\add*? For I testify to you that, if it had been possible, you would have dug out your eyes and given them to me.
\v 16 So have I become your enemy by telling you the truth?
\v 17 They are zealous over you \add [i.e. they court you and make much of you]\add*, but not for good; they want to shut you out \add [i.e. to leave you standing outside until you come to them for the one thing they say you lack]\add*, so that you may be zealous over them.
\v 18 It is good to be sought with zeal in a good thing always, and not only when I am present with you —
\v 19 my little children, for whom I am again \add [i.e. as I was when you first came to birth]\add* in birth pains until the Christ is formed in you \add [i.e. until he has taken shape within you — which is the opposite of a shape cut into you from outside]\add*!
\v 20 I could wish to be present with you now and to change my voice \add [i.e. a letter has one tone only; face to face I could soften it or sharpen it as I read your faces]\add*, because I am at a loss about you.
\p
\v 21 Tell me, you who want to be under Torah, do you not hear the Torah \add [i.e. have you listened to what it actually says?]\add*?
\v 22 For it is written that Abraham had two sons, one from the slave woman and one from the free woman.\f + \fr 4:22 \ft Genesis 16 and 21: Hagar the Egyptian slave and her son Ishmael, Sarah the wife and her son Isaac.\f*
\v 23 But the one from the slave woman was born according to flesh \add [i.e. by Abraham and Sarah's own arrangement, when they set about producing the promised heir themselves]\add*, while the one from the free woman was born through a promise \add [i.e. from a barren womb, by God's word and nothing else]\add*.
\v 24 These things are spoken as an allegory\f + \fr 4:24 \ft Greek \tl allēgoreō\tl*, to read an account for a meaning it carries beyond the events themselves. It is not a denial that the events happened.\f*: these women are two covenants. One is from Mount Sinai, bearing children into slavery — she is Hagar.
\v 25 Now Hagar is Mount Sinai in Arabia; she stands in the same line with the present Jerusalem \add [i.e. the city as it now stands, and the covenant establishment centered there, from which the men troubling you take their authority]\add*, for she is in slavery with her children.
\v 26 But the Jerusalem above \add [i.e. the city that is God's own, not the one now standing]\add* is free, and she is our mother.
\v 27 For it is written, "Be glad, barren one who does not bear; break out and shout, you who are not in birth pains; because many are the children of the desolate one, more than of her who has the husband."\f + \fr 4:27 \ft Isaiah 54:1. The method that looks barren — promise, with no human machinery behind it — will fill the house; the method that looks fruitful will not.\f*
\p
\v 28 But you, brothers, are children of promise, in the manner of Isaac.
\v 29 But just as then the one born according to flesh hunted down the one born according to Spirit\f + \fr 4:29 \ft Genesis 21:9 says only that Ishmael was "playing" with Isaac; Jewish reading of the verse took the word as mockery and hostility.\f*, so it is now also \add [i.e. the men pressing circumcision on you stand where Ishmael stood, and you where Isaac stood]\add*.
\v 30 But what does the Scripture say \add [— the very Torah they want you under]\add*? "Cast out the slave woman and her son, for the son of the slave woman shall never inherit with the son of the free woman."\f + \fr 4:30 \ft Genesis 21:10. In the account these are Sarah's words; Paul cites them as Scripture's own verdict.\f*
\v 31 So then, brothers, we are not children of a slave woman but of the free woman.

\c 5
\p
\v 1 For freedom the Christ set us free. Stand firm, then, and do not be held again in a yoke of slavery.\f + \fr 5:1 \ft Among the teachers, "the yoke of the Torah" was a good thing, gladly taken up. Laid on Gentiles as the price of standing before God, Paul says, the same yoke is a yoke of slavery.\f*
\p
\v 2 Look — I, Paul, say to you that if you are circumcised \add [i.e. if you take it as the thing that secures your standing before God]\add*, the Christ will be of no use to you \add [i.e. you will have moved onto ground where what he did does not operate]\add*.
\v 3 And I testify again to every man who is circumcised that he is a debtor to do the whole Torah \add [i.e. all of it, and not the one rite alone — circumcision is the door into the entire covenant, and the whole obligation comes through the door with you]\add*.
\v 4 You were cut loose from the Christ, you who are being set right in Torah \add [i.e. you who are seeking your standing inside that covenant]\add*; you fell out of the favor \add [— the two are not halves of one thing; to step onto the one is to step off the other]\add*.
\v 5 For we, by Spirit, out of faithfulness, are waiting eagerly for the hope of righteousness \add [i.e. the vindication still ahead of us — and we wait for it by the same Spirit and trust we began with, not by some later means]\add*.
\v 6 For in the Christ Jesus neither circumcision nor uncircumcision has any strength \add [i.e. neither the cut nor the uncut counts for anything whatever]\add*, but faithfulness working through love.
\p
\v 7 You were running well. Who cut in on you \add [i.e. broke into your track and broke your stride]\add*, so that you are not persuaded by the truth?
\v 8 This persuading is not from the one who calls you \add [i.e. from God, who called you and calls you still]\add*.
\v 9 A little leaven leavens the whole lump.\f + \fr 5:9 \ft A household proverb. Leaven was the standing picture of what spreads and cannot be confined; before Passover every crumb of it was cleared from the house (Exodus 12:15).\f*
\v 10 I am persuaded about you in the Lord that you will think nothing else; but the one stirring you up will carry the judgment, whoever he may be \add [— his standing makes no difference, any more than mine would, or an angel's]\add*.
\v 11 But I, brothers — if I still proclaim circumcision \add [as they are saying I do]\add*, why am I still being hunted down? In that case the stumbling-block of the cross \add [i.e. its offense, which is its claim to be enough by itself]\add* has been done away with.
\v 12 I wish those who are turning you upside down would cut themselves clean off!\f + \fr 5:12 \ft The wish cuts two ways. If cutting flesh gains standing before God, let them go the whole way and see. And a man so cut is barred by Deuteronomy 23:1 from the very assembly they claim to be guarding.\f*
\p
\v 13 For you were called to freedom, brothers — only not freedom as an opening for the flesh\f + \fr 5:13 \ft Greek \tl aphormē\tl*, a military term: the fortified position from which a campaign is launched.\f*, but through love be slaves to one another \add [— the one slavery I will lay on you]\add*.
\v 14 For the whole Torah has been filled full in one word, in this: "You shall love your neighbor as yourself."\f + \fr 5:14 \ft Leviticus 19:18. Paul quotes the Torah as the sum of the Torah. What he refuses is entry into its covenant as the ground of standing before God; its instruction he teaches and commands.\f*
\v 15 But if you bite and devour one another \add [— which is what this quarrel has already made of you]\add*, watch out that you are not consumed by one another.
\p
\v 16 But I say, walk by Spirit \add [i.e. let him set your course, step by step]\add*, and you will never carry out the craving of the flesh.
\v 17 For the flesh craves against the Spirit, and the Spirit against the flesh; for these are set against one another, so that you may not do the things you want \add [i.e. you cannot simply follow your own wanting — one of the two has you]\add*.
\v 18 But if you are led by Spirit, you are not under Torah \add [i.e. not under that covenant's jurisdiction; the Spirit works from inside you what the Torah commanded from outside]\add*.
\p
\v 19 Now the works of the flesh \add [i.e. what that whole way of living turns out]\add* are plain to see: sexual immorality, uncleanness, wantonness,
\v 20 idolatry, sorcery\f + \fr 5:20 \ft Greek \tl pharmakeia\tl*, the use of drugs and spells to bend the unseen powers.\f*, hostilities, strife, jealousy, outbursts of anger, self-seeking rivalries, dissensions, factions,
\v 21 envyings, drunkenness, carousings, and things like these \add [i.e. the list is a sample, not a boundary]\add* — which I tell you beforehand, just as I told you before, that those who practice such things \add [i.e. who live that way, not who stumble once]\add* will not inherit the kingdom of God \add [i.e. will have no share in the age to come]\add*.
\p
\v 22 But the fruit of the Spirit\f + \fr 5:22 \ft The flesh has "works," many and scattered; the Spirit has "fruit," one thing with these nine faces. The one is manufactured, the other grown.\f* is love, joy, peace, long-suffering, kindness, goodness, faithfulness \add [i.e. being reliable — a man whose word holds]\add*,
\v 23 gentleness, self-control. Against such things there is no law \add [i.e. no law was ever written against these — the Spirit yields more than the Torah could require]\add*.
\v 24 And those who are of the Christ Jesus crucified the flesh with its passions and cravings \add [i.e. it went to the cross with him; its right to rule you is broken]\add*.
\p
\v 25 If we live by Spirit, let us also walk in line by Spirit.\f + \fr 5:25 \ft The verb is from marching in formation: keeping rank, keeping step, following the pace that is set.\f*
\v 26 Let us not become hungry for empty glory, provoking one another, envying one another.

\c 6
\p
\v 1 Brothers, if a man is even caught in some misstep \add [i.e. overtaken by it before he saw it coming — not a man who has settled into it]\add*, you who are spiritual \add [i.e. any of you who are walking by the Spirit]\add* — mend\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Greek \tl katartizō\tl*, used of setting a broken bone and of mending torn nets. The work is repair, not punishment.\f* such a one in a spirit of gentleness, keeping your eye on yourself \add [— you stand where he stood, and no higher]\add*, so that you too are not put to the test.
\v 2 Carry one another's heavy loads, and in this way you will fill full the Torah of the Christ \add [i.e. the command to love your neighbor, in the shape the Christ himself gave it when he handed himself over for us]\add*.
\v 3 For if anyone thinks he is something when he is nothing, he deceives his own mind.
\v 4 But let each one test his own work, and then he will have the boast toward himself alone and not toward the other \add [i.e. measured against his own work, never against his neighbor's]\add*;
\v 5 for each one will carry his own pack.\f + \fr 6:5 \ft Two different words. Verse 2 is a load too heavy for one man, which is why the others take hold of it. Here it is the soldier's own pack, which no one can carry in his place.\f*
\p
\v 6 And let the one who is taught the word share in all good things \add [i.e. support him out of what you have]\add* with the one who teaches.
\p
\v 7 Do not be led astray: God is not sneered at\f + \fr 6:7 \ft The Greek verb is literally "to turn up the nose" — the gesture of open contempt.\f*. For whatever a man sows, that he will also reap;
\v 8 because the one who sows into his own flesh \add [i.e. and the knife they are urging on you is an investment in flesh if anything is]\add* will reap decay out of the flesh, but the one who sows into the Spirit will reap the life of the age \add [i.e. the life of the age to come]\add* out of the Spirit.
\v 9 And let us not lose heart in doing good, for in its own season we will reap, if we do not go slack \add [— the harvest is certain, but not to the man who lets go of the plough]\add*.
\v 10 So then, as we have opportunity, let us work what is good toward all, and especially toward those of the household of the faith \add [i.e. the household marked out by trust, not by circumcision]\add*.
\p
\v 11 See with what large letters I have written to you with my own hand.\f + \fr 6:11 \ft The letter to this point was taken down by a secretary. From here Paul writes himself, in an outsized hand that makes the closing impossible to overlook.\f*
\v 12 As many as want to put on a good face in flesh \add [i.e. to have circumcised Gentiles to show]\add*, these are the ones compelling you to be circumcised — only so that they may not be hunted down for the cross of the Christ \add [— that is the whole of their reason]\add*.
\v 13 For not even those who are being circumcised keep Torah themselves \add [— they press on you an obligation they do not carry]\add*, but they want you to be circumcised so that they may boast in your flesh \add [i.e. count your cut bodies as their trophies]\add*.
\v 14 But may it never be that I boast, except in the cross of our Lord Jesus the Christ, through whom the world \add [i.e. the whole order in which such marks confer standing]\add* has been crucified to me, and I to the world.
\v 15 For neither is circumcision anything nor uncircumcision, but a new creation\f + \fr 6:15 \ft The language of the prophets' promised remaking: Isaiah 43:18-19; 65:17. Not the old order improved, but a new act of God on the scale of the first creation.\f* \add [i.e. that, and nothing done to the body, is what counts now]\add*.
\v 16 And as many as will walk in line by this rule — peace upon them and mercy, and upon the Israel of God.\f + \fr 6:16 \ft "And upon the Israel of God" may add a second group — Jewish believers, blessed alongside all who walk by this rule — or may name the whole believing community as God's Israel. Either way it stands apart from "the present Jerusalem" of 4:25.\f*
\p
\v 17 From now on let no one hand me troubles, for I carry in my body the brand-marks of Jesus.\f + \fr 6:17 \ft Greek \tl stigmata\tl*, the brand burned into a slave to show whose he was. Paul's are the scars of beatings and stonings — the only marks in flesh he will exhibit, over against the mark the agitators want cut into the Galatians.\f*
\p
\v 18 The favor of our Lord Jesus the Christ be with your spirit, brothers. Amen.
